\[\begin{split} f(x)&=\frac{2}{\omega}\phi\left(\frac{x- \xi}{\omega}\right)\Phi\left(\alpha\left(\frac{x-\xi}{\omega}\right)\right)\\ \phi(x)&=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{x^{2}}{2}}\\ \Phi(x)&=\int_{-\infty}^{x}\phi(t)dt=\frac{1}{2} \left[1+\text{erf}\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\right]\end{split} \tag{3.22}\]

After the fitting, the yield of signal and background can be obtained by the integration of the signal and background function over the mass region. They are used to calculate the fractions in Equation(3.21). With the raw asymmetries and the fractions, one can get the asymmetry of the \(\pi^{0}\) and background by simply solving Equation(3.21).

#### Jet Transverse Single Spin Asymmetry

The TSSA for jet can be calculated using the method just mentioned. Only the object is the jet instead of the \(\pi^{0}\). Therefore no background subtraction is needed. Note that as a forward detector, there is no tracker coverage, it is possible that there are very few FMS points in the detector. In that case, even one or two FMS points can be reconstructed as a jet event. In this analysis, there is

